{% from "components/five-star-rating-view/macro.njk" import fiveStarRatingView %}

<h2>Ratings at different values</h2>
<p>0 / 5: {{ fiveStarRatingView({ value: 0, label: "Rating: 0 out of 5 stars" }) }}</p>
<p>1 / 5: {{ fiveStarRatingView({ value: 1, label: "Rating: 1 out of 5 stars" }) }}</p>
<p>2 / 5: {{ fiveStarRatingView({ value: 2, label: "Rating: 2 out of 5 stars" }) }}</p>
<p>3 / 5: {{ fiveStarRatingView({ value: 3, label: "Rating: 3 out of 5 stars" }) }}</p>
<p>4 / 5: {{ fiveStarRatingView({ value: 4, label: "Rating: 4 out of 5 stars" }) }}</p>
<p>5 / 5: {{ fiveStarRatingView({ value: 5, label: "Rating: 5 out of 5 stars" }) }}</p>

<h2>Half ratings</h2>
<p>3.5 / 5: {{ fiveStarRatingView({ value: 3.5, label: "Rating: 3.5 out of 5 stars" }) }}</p>
<p>4.2 / 5: {{ fiveStarRatingView({ value: 4.2, label: "Rating: 4.2 out of 5 stars" }) }}</p>

<h2>Accessibility</h2>
<p>The view has <code>role="img"</code> and a descriptive
  <code>aria-label</code> like "Rating: 4 out of 5 stars" so screen
  readers read the value regardless of the visual stars.</p>

{# BEGIN generated: examples (bin/generate-examples) #}
<h2>Usage</h2>
<pre tabindex="0"><code>{% raw %}&lt;span class="five-star-rating-view" role="img" aria-label="3 out of 5 stars"&gt;★★★☆☆&lt;/span&gt;
{% endraw %}</code></pre>
